<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="cowboy-fp" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Cowboy Frying Pan Bread</title>

  <p>
    Ingredients
  </p>
  <tabular>
    <row>
      <cell>2 cups flour</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>2 tablespoon baking soda</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>1 \frac{1}{2}</m> teaspoons salt</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
    <cell><m>1 \frac{1}{4}</m> cup sugar</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}</m> cup oil</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Water</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Butter or honey</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

  <p>Instructions
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Mix flour, baking powder, salt, and sugar in a large bowl.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Gradually stir in the oil and just enough water to make a light dough.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Knead the dough just enough to mix ingredients.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Grease a 7- or 8-inch frying pan.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Cut off enough dough to spread it <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>-inch thick in the bottom of the frying pan.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Poke a hole about 1 inch wide in the middle of the dough to let the steam escape.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Place pan on a stove and cook until bottom of bread is browned.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Set pan in a vertical position in front of the fire.  Be careful that bread doesn't slip out.  Bread needs to face the fire.  Turn pan so top browns evenly.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          When bread is evenly browned, remove it from pan and serve warm with butter or honey.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    Notes:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</section>